\subsection{RQ2: Authority Resurrection}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Token reuse success rate (\%) after restore, by external validation mode.}
\label{tab:tokenreuse}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Validation Mode} & \textbf{Reuse Rate} & \textbf{Attack Window} \\
\midrule
Stateless (JWT, no revocation check) & 94.2 $\pm$ 2.1 & Until expiry \\
Stateful (immediate sync) & 2.4 $\pm$ 1.4 & $<$100ms \\
Stateful (async, 5s propagation) & 31.6 $\pm$ 4.1 & 0--5s \\
Stateful (async, 30s propagation) & 58.4 $\pm$ 4.4 & 0--30s \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:tokenreuse} evaluates authority resurrection under different external validation models.

\textbf{Key finding 4: Stateless validation is highly vulnerable.} With stateless JWT validation (no revocation list), 94.2\% of consumed tokens are accepted after restore. The restored agent simply replays the token from its checkpointed memory.

\textbf{Key finding 5: Propagation delays create attack windows.} Even stateful systems with asynchronous revocation propagation show significant vulnerability. A 30-second propagation delay yields 58.4\% reuse success, representing a realistic attack window in distributed systems.

\textbf{Key finding 6: Immediate synchronous validation is effective.} Only synchronous, immediate revocation checking reduces reuse to 2.4\% (residual cases due to race conditions at checkpoint boundaries).

\subsection{RQ3: Fork-Based Amplification}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Resource consumption under fork-based attacks (quota: 3 VMs/minute).}
\label{tab:forking}
\begin{tabular}{cccc}
\toprule
\textbf{Fork Count} & \textbf{VMs Created} & \textbf{Amplification} & \textbf{Quota Exceeded} \\
\midrule
1 & 2.8 $\pm$ 0.4 & 0.93$\times$ & 0\% \\
