\textbf{Key finding 9: P2 maximizes duplication.} In-flight checkpoints (P2) produce the highest duplication because the external action commits but the agent state does not reflect completion.

\textbf{Key finding 10: P3 reduces token reuse.} Post-response checkpoints (P3) show lower token reuse (42.8\% vs $>$89\%) because the agent may have already processed the ``token consumed'' response before checkpoint, depending on exact timing.

\textbf{Key finding 11: Amplification is injection-independent.} Fork amplification remains consistent ($\sim$5.5$\times$) across injection points because it depends on concurrent execution rather than checkpoint timing.

\subsection{Comparison with Baselines}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Duplicate rates (\%) across checkpoint-restore mechanisms (W1-Payment, P2).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{lcc}
\toprule
\textbf{Mechanism} & \textbf{Duplicate Rate} & \textbf{Key Instability} \\
\midrule
No-CR & 0.0 & N/A \\
Det-Replay & 3.2 $\pm$ 1.6 & 0.0 \\
LG-TimeTravel & 72.6 $\pm$ 3.9 & 69.4 $\pm$ 4.1 \\
CRIU & 78.4 $\pm$ 3.8 & 71.4 $\pm$ 4.0 \\
Podman & 76.8 $\pm$ 3.7 & 70.2 $\pm$ 4.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares checkpoint-restore mechanisms.

\textbf{Key finding 12: Deterministic replay is effective.} Det-Replay achieves near-zero duplication (3.2\%, residual due to network errors) by replaying recorded tool calls instead of re-synthesizing. This validates the durable execution assumption~\cite{temporal} but requires infrastructure changes to record and replay LLM decisions.

\textbf{Key finding 13: Framework and OS-level checkpoints are equally vulnerable.} LangGraph time-travel (72.6\%) shows similar vulnerability to CRIU (78.4\%) and Podman (76.8\%). The vulnerability stems from LLM non-determinism, not the checkpoint mechanism itself.

\subsection{Summary of Findings}

Our evaluation demonstrates that semantic rollback vulnerabilities are:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Frequent}: Duplication rates exceed 64\% for idempotency-enabled services and 89\% for services without idempotency
    \item \textbf{Cross-cutting}: Both OS-level (CRIU, Podman) and application-level (LangGraph) checkpoints exhibit similar vulnerability
    \item \textbf{Exploitable}: Authority resurrection succeeds in 94\% of cases with stateless validation; fork amplification reliably exceeds quotas
    \item \textbf{Mitigatable}: Deterministic replay reduces duplication to 3\%; synchronous stateful validation reduces token reuse to 2\%
\end{enumerate}

These findings confirm a fundamental semantic gap: checkpoint-restore mechanisms designed for deterministic programs produce security violations when applied to non-deterministic LLM agents interacting with stateful external services.

