\begin{abstract}
Checkpoint-restore mechanisms are critical primitives used broadly for fault tolerance, incremental exploration, and debugging of software systems. Their integration into large-language-model (LLM) agents, which dynamically synthesize execution plans and security-critical decisions from natural-language prompts, has received limited attention despite growing deployment. Recent systems work argues that agentic exploration fundamentally needs fast branching, backtracking, and checkpoint-restore support~\cite{agenticexploration}, while production agent frameworks already expose persistent checkpoints and time travel to resume or fork past executions~\cite{langgraph}. We show that na\"ively applying traditional checkpoint-restore primitives to LLM-based agents introduces a class of security vulnerabilities we term \textbf{semantic rollback attacks}. Unlike traditional workflows with deterministic replay semantics, LLM agents inherently produce non-deterministic yet semantically equivalent actions upon restoration, causing mismatches between internal agent state and external irreversible system states.

We systematically define, characterize, and experimentally demonstrate three key semantic rollback vulnerabilities: (1) \textbf{Authority Resurrection}, wherein revoked or expired credentials are unintentionally revived upon restoration; (2) \textbf{Action Duplication due to Identity Instability}, where semantically identical but structurally divergent restored requests bypass idempotency protections, causing repeated external side-effects such as payments or resource provisioning; and (3) \textbf{Security-Context Rollback}, in which previously committed security decisions and approvals are silently reverted.

To demonstrate and measure these vulnerabilities, we present \textbf{AgentRollback}, an evaluation toolkit that triggers real checkpoint-restore using CRIU~\cite{criu} or Podman, combined with external tool services maintaining append-only logs. Our experiments show frequent duplication of external irreversible actions and authorization violations even under benign restoration scenarios. These findings reveal a semantic gap in current checkpoint-restore integrations for LLM agents.
\end{abstract}

